## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical implementation of order-statistics data structures with batched updates. It expects a solution that balances fast batch updates with O(1) median queries by maintaining the median explicitly.

## Key Concepts
- Two balanced structures (lower and upper halves) to partition the data
- Lazy/deferred deletions: mark elements as deleted rather than removing them immediately, deferring cleanup to future operations
- Amortized analysis for batch updates and overall complexity
- Trade-offs between simple heaps and BST-based structures

## Code Example
```python
import heapq

class MedianTracker:
    def __init__(self):
        self.low = []   # max-heap (negatives)
        self.high = []  # min-heap
        self.deleted = set()

    def _cleanup(self):
        while self.low and -self.low[0] in self.deleted:
            self.deleted.remove(-heapq.heappop(self.low))
        while self.high and self.high[0] in self.deleted:
            self.deleted.remove(heapq.heappop(self.high))

    def insert(self, x):
        if not self.low or x <= -self.low[0]:
            heapq.heappush(self.low, -x)
        else:
            heapq.heappush(self.high, x)
        self._rebalance()
        self._cleanup()

    def delete(self, x):
        self.deleted.add(x)
        self._cleanup()

    def _rebalance(self):
        if len(self.low) < len(self.high):
            heapq.heappush(self.low, -heapq.heappop(self.high))
        elif len(self.low) - len(self.high) > 1:
            heapq.heappush(self.high, -heapq.heappop(self.low))

    def median_at_least(self, T):
        self._cleanup()
        return -self.low[0] >= T if self.low else False
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design efficient streaming algorithms that trade precision for memory and speed when computing quantiles—a fundamental requirement in real-time monitoring systems.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming quantile estimation using fixed-bin histograms
- Memory-accuracy trade-offs in one-pass algorithms
- Update complexity O(1) per element, query complexity O(K)
- Error analysis and parameter selection (K vs accuracy)
- Comparison with exact solutions using balanced search trees

## Code Example
```javascript
class FixedBinQuantile {
  constructor(M, K) {
    this.M = M;
    this.K = K;
    this.binWidth = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(M / K));
    this.bins = new Array(K).fill(0);
    this.total = 0;
  }

  add(x) {
    const idx = Math.min(this.K - 1, Math.floor(x / this.binWidth));
    this.bins[idx]++;
    this.total++;
  }

  getPercentile(p) {
    const target = Math.ceil(p * this.total);
    let cumulative = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < this.K; i++) {
      cumulative += this.bins[i];
      if (cumulative >= target) {
        return (i + 1) * this.binWidth; // Upper bound of bin
      }
    }
    return this.M;
  }
}
```

